WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.500
Reuters writes that because of the war in the Middle East a prolonged inflation shock may occur,

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:12.500
government bond yields are rising rapidly and reaching their highest levels in the past ten years,

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:19.000
and all this inevitably leads to serious blows to the purchasing power of states, companies and households.

4
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:28.500
About what may be happening with Serbia, our bonds and the state's further borrowing, I'll talk today with Nenad Gujaničić, chief broker at Momentum Securities.

5
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.500
But first — a quick news roundup.

6
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:43.300
Banks in Serbia expect tightening of pricing conditions for household loans in the second quarter, due to rising interest margins, writes Biznis.rs

7
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.000
based on a survey by the National Bank of Serbia.

8
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:59.500
In the first quarter overall banks slightly tightened standards for long-term loans to the economy, while standards for short-term loans remained unchanged.

9
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:11.000
According to Reuters analysis, in total the war in the Middle East has cost companies worldwide at least 25 billion dollars, and this sum grows with each day of fighting.

10
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:32.500
The analysis shows that at least 279 companies directly named the war as the reason for introducing protective measures to soften the financial blow. These measures include raising product prices, cutting production, halting dividend payments, as well as harsher steps like laying off employees and asking for urgent government aid.

11
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:43.500
The average interest rate at which the G7 countries, the most developed economies, borrow money is approaching 4%. Before the war in the Middle East it was 3.2%.

12
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:51.500
The yield on ten-year US government bonds is now around 4.6%, the highest in more than a year.

13
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:58.200
Why is this a cause for concern among investors and how will Serbia cope in the new circumstances?

14
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.500
That is, what is it sensible for us to do?

15
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:15.500
As I said at the beginning, I'm discussing this with broker Nenad Gujaničić.

16
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:23.500
Nenad, thank you very much for being here and coming to talk about this important topic. It's important globally and, of course, important for us here in Serbia.

17
00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:29.300
Let's first look at why nobody is buying our debt — bonds in dinars.

18
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.700
It can't be said that nobody is buying.

19
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:45.900
The fact is that demand in the domestic debt market has fallen since the end of March, I think, when the second-to-last auction was held, for the simple reason that inflationary pressures intensified, expectations of rate hikes intensified, as you said,

20
00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:54.900
so domestic banks, the main buyers of domestic debt, slowed down a bit, and in that sense the last two auctions, let's say, were rather sluggish.

21
00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:02.100
What our state does, of course, is above all borrow on the foreign market, that is, issue eurobonds,

22
00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:11.300
which in the meantime have been successfully placed, and that's how our state mainly borrows. The domestic market is small, it plugs some hole, but the main part of the debt is government bonds on the international market.

23
00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:25.060
Well, then let's analyze what's in foreign currencies, what's in dinars — why is there such a big inflow of demand for our bonds in foreign currencies?

24
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:37.500
Let's first draw a distinction. In general, whenever bonds of a domestic issuer are issued on the international market, it's used as a kind of advertising weapon: look, there was huge demand.

25
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
You can actually sell as much of this as you like. That is, these aren't some super favorable conditions from the issuer's point of view.

26
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:54.300
We're small on the international market. Whether you're called Telekom or the Republic of Serbia, at the rates foreigners give you, you can borrow as much as you like.

27
00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:10.520
So saying that demand was several times higher — in my opinion is above all some populist advertising for the domestic average citizen. Try borrowing at 1% more expensive, and let's see what demand would have been.

28
00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:19.340
So this element can safely be excluded. As for our state, for more than ten, maybe fifteen years now,

29
00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:28.600
on the international market these funds are actually raised relatively easily. We're a recognizable issuer, that is, we didn't enter the debt market yesterday.

30
00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:42.080
In the end, all these buyers of new bonds are holders of the previous debt. If you saw, in this last issue there was also a buyback of part of the bond maturing in 2027.

31
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:47.960
Probably some old buyers said: buy these back from us so we can extend the term a bit with some new bond.

32
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.300
That is, it's a fairly routine process.

33
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:59.720
The price of our debt is close to 5%, and that's about half a percentage point more than a month or two ago.

34
00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.960
This is the price of ten-year bonds.

35
00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:06.420
And what's the difference when we borrow in dinars and when in foreign currency?

36
00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:14.900
It's better for us to borrow in local currency, for the simple reason that we can manage our debt relatively easily.

37
00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:21.280
Why in the past did we have many issues in dinars and place them successfully?

38
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:32.960
Because we have a stable, not to say fixed, dinar exchange rate, foreigners were looking for some real return, and when there's a real return — it's the nominal return minus exchange-rate slippage — then you get a real return.

39
00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:46.680
Issues in dinars at 5% for the foreign, and also for the domestic investor, and 5% in euros — that's quite attractive, considering that we've had a stable rate for about eight or nine years, let's say, relatively stable,

40
00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:53.120
and so foreign investors, and also domestic ones, who are fewer, readily agreed to this game. You can also take domestic citizens,

41
00:05:53.120 --> 00:06:01.340
who save more in dinars: now, for example, savings in dinars make up about 10% of all savings, and that's an absolute historic record,

42
00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:14.080
if only because we have a fixed rate, and the nominal return is at the same time the real one, and this exchange-rate risk, I'm afraid, is poorly accounted for — both among our citizens and among international investors.

43
00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:22.420
Speaking of the current conditions the whole world is in, and we too, why should the state keep borrowing in such conditions and also present it as some kind of success?

44
00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:32.760
It's unavoidable. That is, if your level of public debt is about 40 billion euros, you have annual interest of around 2–2.5 billion euros,

45
00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:41.520
you must borrow to refinance the previous debt that falls due, pay interest, and let's not forget, we have a budget deficit,

46
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:53.340
although we're in a golden age, the budget deficit is not small, not extremely large, but if you have a budget minus, you have to cover it somehow: either you sell assets, or you borrow, there is no third way.

47
00:06:54.040 --> 00:07:00.940
How will we pay these old debts if they come due with a smaller GDP and generally with higher inflation than expected?

48
00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:10.440
When we borrow, two things matter. The first is the price of the debt, and the second, no less important, one could say even more important, is how these funds are used.

49
00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:20.520
It's not the same: taking a consumer loan and going to the seaside — or investing in some entrepreneurial venture of yours, in liquidity, or paying for education and so on.

50
00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:41.020
I'm afraid most of our debt, apart from servicing previous debts, goes rather irrationally, because we've heard more than once from our state officials that we have no feasibility studies and that a mass of this debt goes "by eye" into various infrastructure projects, about which it's a question whether they paid off.

51
00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:49.540
And this is our biggest problem. If you look at the relative level of debt in relation to GDP, it's not dramatic, in fact it looks quite attractive,

52
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:56.520
but I'm afraid if we broke down this debt, which is rather inflated and not sustainable, because our economic model is not sustainable,

53
00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:03.780
we'd arrive at a rather alarming situation where in the future we'll have higher interest at the annual level,

54
00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.240
and we'll finance it with increasing difficulty — for the simple reason that such economic growth in this form is not sustainable.

55
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:20.120
What will we do? We'll keep borrowing. What the conditions will be — those conditions during and after the pandemic, exceptionally favorable, can be forgotten.

56
00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:30.200
If they stay at this level, it will be acceptable, but, I repeat, this debt spiral can arise relatively quickly, and then it will be very hard to get out of it.

57
00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:48.000
If you have domestic debt, debt in the national currency, it goes somewhat easier, but I must remind you that most of our debts are in the hands of foreigners or of domestic banks, which mostly belong to foreigners, so that complicates our position,

58
00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:59.120
and this is one of the biggest shortcomings of the current government, and the previous one too, one can say: it did nothing to get domestic citizens and the domestic economy onto the financial market,

59
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:02.360
so that they would at least to some extent be owners of our debt.

60
00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:12.400
Of course nothing came of this. We know that domestic citizens hold only in banks about 17 billion euros, and the number of citizens buying domestic government bonds is extremely small.

61
00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.200
Nenad, thank you very much for this analysis.

62
00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:15.180
Thanks for the invitation.

63
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:21.100
Broker Nenad Gujaničić was our guest today. Watch us tomorrow too at 11 o'clock on the YouTube channel

64
00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:26.780
"New Economy." Leave a comment, hit like, you can also subscribe to our channel.

65
00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.340
Thank you in any case for watching us. Have a nice day.